removed from scope → RHAISTRAT-7824 — AC-3 descoped; scoping model resolved to per-namespace.\n5. Performance thresholds dropped; order-of-magnitude benchmark + flat scan — AC shape on E003.\n6. Platform shared PostgreSQL via DSC not until 3.6 → self-provisioning for 3.5, portability requirement on branch epics.\n7. RHAISTRAT-7164 hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning → go/no-go Investigation E001 with branches.\n8. BFF-managed alternative → branch B.\n9. Remote vector store implementation reusable → E002 scoped small; most effort in provisioning.\n\nComponent mapping notes:\n- \"Gen AI Studio\" canonical component used for E001/E003/branch epics; work occurs in the odh-dashboard repository's gen-ai BFF and (branch A) the modularized dashboard operator layer — noted as span.\n- \"OGX Core\" used for E002; the ogx-distribution is the relevant surface.\n\nNow write epic bodies. Remember: no sibling-epic references in bodies (no \"E001\", \"E002\", \"E003\", \"E004\"). Must describe dependencies in plain terms. Referencing RHAISTRAT-7164 etc. is fine.\n\nE001 body:\n# Title: Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go\n\n## Description\nInvestigation to decide how the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio playground RAG is provisioned in RHOAI 3.5... three bounded outcomes: operator-based self-provisioning via the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164); BFF-managed provisioning by the gen-ai BFF; or requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL. Define the go/no-go gate criteria as part of sprint planning, assess feasibility of each, produce a decision record.\n\n## Scope\n- Define go/no-go gate criteria (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing vs 3.5 dates)\n- Assess BFF-managed provisioning lifecycle feasibility: cleanup on playground deletion, pod restart handling, credential rotation (BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop)\n- Confirm per-namespace scoping (Auto Rag decoupled)\n- Evaluate portability requirement: consume platform-provided shared PostgreSQL connection string when available (RHOAI 3.6)\n- Decision record with selected approach and fallback\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Gate criteria documented and the go/no-go decision recorded with rationale\n- For BFF-managed option: lifecycle risks (cleanup, restarts, credential rotation) assessed with mitigation or judged unworkable\n- For operator-based option: RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery assessment against target release dates\n- Decision identifies exactly one of the three outcomes; downstream plans updated\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- AC-1 (default vector store OOB): this investigation determines the provisioning approach...\n- SC-3 ...\n\nE002 body:\n# Enable the pgvector remote vector_io provider in OGX\n\n## Description\nDeliver the OGX-side pgvector vector I/O provider integration... reuse existing remote vector store implementation; configure remote::pgvector via ENABLE_PGVECTOR; separate process from the OGX instance; app storage untouched (SQLite).\n\n## Scope\n- Adapt/verify the existing remote vector store implementation for the pgvector backend\n- Provider activation via the OGX vector_io provider configuration (remote::pgvector), guarded by its env var, with connection details supplied via secret-backed configuration\n- Verify vector I/O operations (register/insert/query documents) against a PostgreSQL instance with pgvector\n- Keep application storage backend configuration (kv/sql) untouched\n- Document the provider configuration surface for consumers wiring OGXServer instances\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- pgvector vector_io provider activates when configured and vector register/insert/query operations succeed against a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance\n- Vector store runs as a separate process decoupled from the OGX process (no inline provider)\n- Application storage backend remains on its existing default; no storage migration involved\n- Configuration surface (env/secret references) documented for downstream consumers\n\n## HLR Traceability: AC-2, AC-4, AC-5.\n\nE003 body:\n# Wire gen-ai BFF playground RAG to the default pgvector vector store\n\n## Description\nWire the gen-ai BFF so playgrounds with RAG use the default pgvector vector store... configure OGXServer instances with the pgvector vector_io provider, consume the provisioned PostgreSQL connection, validate end-to-end, benchmark vs inline Milvus baseline, resource sizing.\n\n## Scope\n- When creating OGXServer instances for playgrounds with RAG, configure the pgvector vector_io provider (replacing reliance on the removed inline provider)\n- Consume the connection details of the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach (namespace-scoped secret)\n- Keep application storage config as-is\n- End-to-end validation of playground RAG workflows (no loss of current capability)\n- Sprint-1 benchmark: capture inline Milvus baseline, verify pgvector same order of magnitude (no latency SLAs; dev experimentation default; flat scan acceptable)\n- Resource sizing: start from model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjust per benchmark\n- User guidance: production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- OGXServer instances created for RAG-enabled playgrounds have the pgvector vector_io provider configured and connect to the provisioned PostgreSQL instance in the playground namespace\n- Playground RAG workflows (document upload, indexing, retrieval) function end-to-end with no loss of current capability\n- Benchmark report captures inline Milvus baseline and shows pgvector within the same order of magnitude\n- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits documented, based on the model-registry-operator reference and benchmark\n- Per-namespace isolation verified: playgrounds in different namespaces use isolated vector store data\n- Rollback: ... hmm, does this replace existing capability? The inline provider is being removed by RHAISTRAT-7474; this strategy replaces it. Rule 23: strategy replaces existing capability → add rollback/feature-flag AC. The capability being replaced: inline vector store provider. Add an AC: the pgvector provider configuration can be reverted/disabled (config-level toggle) so the previous behavior can be restored if needed. Reasonable: \"The pgvector provider configuration is reversible at the configuration level (the previous inline-provider-based behavior can be restored without code rollback)\". Hmm, but the inline provider is being removed by RHAISTRAT-7474 anyway. Still, Rule 23 says add rollback/feature-flag AC to replacing Implementation epic. I'll add a config-toggle AC.\n\n## HLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-2, AC-5, AC-6 (+ bundled notes).\n\nDependencies (plain terms): the pgvector provider configuration surface delivered by the OGX-side provider integration; the provisioning-approach decision. No epic IDs.\n\nBRANCH-A-E004 body:\n# Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n\n## Description\nIf the go/no-go selects operator-based self-provisioning: implement PostgreSQL lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer for Gen AI Studio (delivered by dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164), following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n\n## Scope\n- PostgreSQL instance lifecycle (create, secret/credentials, cleanup) reconciled by the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer, following the model-registry-operator pattern (controller-created Deployment, Service, Secret per instance)\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector (no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding)\n- Per-namespace isolation for multi-tenant environments\n- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference\n- Portability: consume a platform-provided shared PostgreSQL connection string (DSC-managed, planned for a later release) instead of self-provisioning when available\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The operator reconciles namespace-scoped PostgreSQL instances with pgvector available, including credentials via Secret and cleanup on deletion\n- Instances run with resource requests/limits based on the model-registry-operator reference\n- No custom container image is built; the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector is used\n- Portability documented: switching to the platform-provided shared database requires only consuming a connection string\n- Per-namespace isolation verified\n\n## HLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-4, AC-5, AC-6.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove, fallback \"If the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for the target release, this approach is removed; provisioning falls back to the BFF-managed path or to requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL.\"\n\nBRANCH-B-E004 body:\n# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the gen-ai BFF\n\n## Description\nIf the go/no-go selects BFF-managed provisioning: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, modeled on how it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure.\n\n## Scope\n- When a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret\n- Lifecycle management design: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, credential rotation (the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop — explicit design attention)\n- Portability: when the platform-provided shared database becomes available (later release), consume the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector; per-namespace scoping; resource sizing from model-registry-operator reference\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Provisioning is idempotent: configuring a playground with RAG results in exactly one running pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance in the namespace, reused when already present\n- Lifecycle behaviors validated: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery from pod restarts, documented credential rotation approach\n- Portability documented: consuming a shared platform connection string requires no rewrite of the RAG workflow path\n- Per-namespace isolation verified\n\n## HLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-4, AC-5, AC-6.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=rewrite, fallback \"If BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable, fall back to requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL: users manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their PostgreSQL connection details.\"\n\nNow signals files (one per epic, 5 files). Format: markdown table Signal/Value/Rationale.\n\nNow write everything. Use Write tool for bodies, then frontmatter.py set for FM. Careful with quoting in bash for values with spaces/commas — use single quotes in shell? The tool parses field=value; values with spaces need quoting. I'll use double quotes carefully (escape inner quotes) or single quotes. Note: gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach contains parentheses and commas — single-quote it.\n\nAlso dependencies: \"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001,RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\" — comma-separated; script probably parses to list.\n\nLet me also double check: does E003 need gated_by? \"Every epic that depends on an Investigation should have gated_by pointing to that Investigation\" — E003 depends on E001 → gated_by=E001, and gate_failure_impact (action=rewrite, fallback: BYO outcome). Branch epics: gated_by=E001 with their impacts. E002 doesn't depend on E001.\n\nOne more check on the \"no priority inversion\" and validator's priority check: all P0, fine.\n\nMermaid DAG:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 Provisioning approach go/no-go]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 OGX pgvector provider]\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 BFF wiring + validation]\n    BA[RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004]\n    BB[RHA