{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-17c539b2-a328-46dd-8df8-684c7cc0720d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b070bf7707d21a85","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-g6rwamj0/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n\nParent strategy: RHAISTRAT-8004 — provide a default vector store available out-of-the-box so Gen AI Studio continues to function when inline providers are removed from the Llama Stack distribution (RHAISTRAT-7474).\n\nDecomposition basis: the strategy's full technical section is stored as a Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in this workspace; the decomposition was built from the Summary, Problem Statement, Acceptance Criteria, Open Questions, and the Staff Engineer Input (which takes precedence per Step 1.5). The strategy's Acceptance Criteria serve as the HLR set (AC-1..AC-6). See Health Warnings.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (traceability)\n\nThe Staff Engineer Input diverges from / corrects the AI-generated strategy as follows; each override is reflected in the epic structure:\n\n1. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — OGX defaults to SQLite for application storage; the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite when creating OGXServer instances. Introducing PostgreSQL for vector storage is new work, not an extension of existing infrastructure. → Framed the provisioning work as new work (Investigation + branch Implementations).\n2. **A Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector already exists** (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`; model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it). → No custom image build epic and no Konflux onboarding epic; the strategy's 1-2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated.\n3. **Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations.** Only the pgvector `vector_io` provider is configured; application storage stays on SQLite. → All storage-consolidation and SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration scope removed from epics.\n4. **Decouple from Auto Rag.** Auto Rag compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). → AC-3 (Auto Rag) descoped from this decomposition; the scoping-model open question resolves to per-namespace (per-instance scoping was only needed for Auto Rag).\n5. **Performance thresholds are dev-experimentation scope.** No latency SLAs; the benchmark verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline; flat scan (no index) is sufficient. → Latency/corpus/index open questions removed; benchmark folded into the wiring/validation epic as an acceptance criterion.\n6. **Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC is not available until RHOAI 3.6** — for 3.5, Gen AI Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL. → Self-provisioning is the 3.5 scope; portability to the shared database is an explicit design requirement on the provisioning epics.\n7. **Operator-based self-provisioning has a hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164** (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) and a go/no-go gate must be defined in sprint planning. → Modeled as Investigation E001 with conditional branches.\n8. **BFF-managed provisioning is the alternative fallback** (modeled on the BFF's existing guardrails infrastructure provisioning), with lifecycle and portability caveats. → Modeled as branch B.\n9. **The existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable for pgvector**; most effort is infrastructure/provisioning. → The OGX-side provider epic is scoped small; the provisioning decision carries the structural weight.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable the pgvector remote vector_io provider in OGX | Implementation | OGX Core | OGX Core | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Wire the gen-ai BFF to the default pgvector vector store for playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the gen-ai BFF (branch B) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n\nMain-plan epics: E001-E003. Branch epics BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 are conditional alternatives selected by the outcome of E001; exactly one is instantiated. `epic_count=5` counts all epics including branches.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Provisioning approach go/no-go (Investigation)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 OGX pgvector remote vector_io provider\"]\n    E003[\"E003 gen-ai BFF wiring + E2E validation\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Operator-based provisioning (branch A)\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 BFF-managed provisioning (branch B)\"]\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n    E001 -.->|selects| BA\n    E001 -.->|selects| BB\n```\n\nSolid arrows are main-plan dependency edges (dependency must complete before dependent starts). Dashed arrows are branch-selection gates, not main-plan edges. Critical path length (main plan): 2 (E001 → E003 and E002 → E003).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The provisioning-approach decision determines how the gen-ai BFF obtains and consumes the PostgreSQL connection (operator-created secret vs. BFF-created resources vs. user-provided connection). True gate: `rewrite` if the outcome differs from the assumed approach. E003 carries `gated_by=E001`. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The BFF's OGXServer configuration builds against the pgvector provider configuration surface that E002 delivers and validates (provider activation, secret-backed connection handling). Serial. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 3 (bounded outcome, conditional branch) | Branch A exists only if the go/no-go selects operator-based self-provisioning (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time). `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 (bounded outcome, conditional branch) | Branch B exists only if the go/no-go selects BFF-managed provisioning. `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` with bring-your-own PostgreSQL as the recorded fallback. |\n| No edge E001 → E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | The OGX-side provider integration is identical under every provisioning outcome; the investigation does not change what E002 builds. Different component and team (OGX Core vs. Gen AI Studio). |\n| No edge E002 → branch epics | Rule 13 exception (configuration reference) | The provisioned PostgreSQL instance is consumed by the provider as a connection/secret reference, not built against. The provisioning epics do not depend on the provider surface. |\n| No edge BRANCH-*-E004 → E003 | Branch rule 2 (main-plan epic must not depend on a branch epic) | E003 depends on the *outcome* of E001, not on a specific branch's work. The coupling to the selected provisioning implementation is expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on E003 (end-to-end validation runs against an instance provisioned by the selected approach), never as a DAG edge. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLR set = the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (the strategy's HLR table is in the unavailable attachment; see Health Warnings). All requirements are P0 — the strategy priority is Blocker and RHAISTRAT-7474 is blocked on it.\n\n| HLR | Requirement | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|-------------|----------|---------|-------|\n| AC-1 | Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration of external infrastructure | P0 | E001, E003, BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | E001 selects the provisioning approach; exactly one branch epic is instantiated and delivers the out-of-the-box instance; E003 wires the playground to it. |\n| AC-2 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability | P0 | E002, E003 | Bundled: the E2E validation and the sprint-1 benchmark are folded into E003 (the epic that wires the BFF to the provider) rather than split into a separate validation epic — they exercise the same BFF/provider integration surface, and splitting would force two passes over the same integration and an artificial serialization edge. E002 delivers the provider capability being validated. |\n| AC-3 | Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — | none (descoped) | Staff Engineer Input override: Auto Rag is decoupled from this strategy and addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Intentional traceability gap against the original AC list. |\n| AC-4 | Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not inline) | P0 | E002, BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | E002 enables the remote provider (separate process); the branch epics provision PostgreSQL as its own workload, decoupled from the OGX instance. |\n| AC-5 | Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | P0 | E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | The scoping open question resolves to per-namespace once Auto Rag is removed (per-instance scoping was only motivated by Auto Rag); isolation is verified in E003's E2E validation and in the branch epic. |\n| AC-6 | Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004, E003 | Resource requests/limits start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference (branch epics) and are adjusted based on E003's benchmark against the inline Milvus baseline (order-of-magnitude comparison, no latency SLAs — dev-experimentation default). |\n| SC-1 | No functional regression when inline providers are removed | P0 | E002, E003 | Success criterion covered by AC-2 mapping. |\n| SC-2 | No additional manual setup steps for RAG workflows | P0 | E001, BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004, E003 | Out-of-the-box provisioning plus BFF wiring; the bring-your-own outcome would violate this and is recorded as the fallback of last resort. |\n| SC-3 | RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocked | P0 | all | Whole decomposition delivers the replacement capability RHAISTRAT-7474 requires. |\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the full strategy attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) with the Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, HLR list, Dependencies table, and NFRs is not available in this workspace. The decomposition uses the Acceptance Criteria as the HLR set and infers teams from component ownership and the Staff Engineer Input (Gen AI Studio owns the dashboard/BFF and operator-layer work; OGX Core owns the provider work). Verify team assignments against the full strategy when available.\n- **External dependency with uncertain timing:** RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) gates the operator-based provisioning branch. This is modeled as a conditional branch on the go/no-go Investigation rather than a blocking edge on all work, so the provider integration (E002) proceeds independently (Tier 1). Committing the strategy to a specific release remains risky until RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery is confirmed — the Staff Engineer Input flags this explicitly.\n- **Open question not resolved by Staff Engineer Input:** \"Should users have a migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database?\" The answer does not change which epics exist (the default is scoped to dev experimentation; production-scale users are directed to an external vector database), so it is captured as a portability design requirement on the provisioning epics and user guidance in E003 — not as an Investigation. Follow up in RHAISTRAT-7824 / the RHOAI 3.6 shared-database work.\n- **AC-3 descoped (priority/traceability note):** the Auto Rag acceptance criterion is intentionally uncovered, per Staff Engineer override #4. Reviewers comparing against the original AC list will see a gap by design.\n- **Component mapping notes:** canonical component `Gen AI Studio` is used for E001/E003 and the branch epics; their work spans the odh-dashboard repository (gen-ai BFF, `packages/gen-ai/bff`) and, for branch A, the modularized dashboard operator layer from RHAISTRAT-7164 — `AI Core Dashboard` is the alternative canonical name for the repository, but the domain-specific work is Gen AI Studio's. `OGX Core` maps to the ogx-distribution surface for E002.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe go/no-go branch structure is the tiered delivery for the RHAISTRAT-7164 timing risk:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E002 — the OGX pgvector provider integration. Identical under every provisioning outcome; delivers the reusable provider capability and de-risks the vector_io integration regardless of the decision.\n- **Tier 2 (gated by E001):** the selected provisioning branch epic (BRANCH-A-E004 or BRANCH-B-E004) and the connection-consumption portion of E003. E003's provider-configuration wiring can start in parallel; its E2E validation acceptance criteria gate on a provisioned instance from the selected approach.\n- **Outcome C (bring-your-own PostgreSQL):** requires no provisioning implementation epic. If selected, E003 is rewritten to consume a user-provided PostgreSQL connection from the OGXServer CR spec — recorded in E003's `gate_failure_impact` and as branch B's fallback. This outcome degrades the out-of-the-box experience (SC-2) and is the fallback of last resort.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":65145,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beca486d0051d478","agent_id":"a62e85b504ccecffb","session_id":"95b0e417-f5cf-489c-8110-90a1ec4486c8","uuid":"c1fae4d7-dda7-4e01-8af0-46edfe20b322","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:41:12.393Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
