{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b2993668-8569-49a5-8dbb-3040b763b48f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5558f0eefcb15a7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-g6rwamj0/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation decides how the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio playground RAG is provisioned in RHOAI 3.5, so that a default vector store is available out-of-the-box when inline providers are removed from the Llama Stack distribution. It defines the go/no-go gate criteria (as part of sprint planning), assesses the feasibility of each candidate approach, and produces a decision record that determines which provisioning implementation proceeds. A platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC will not be available until RHOAI 3.6, so for 3.5 Gen AI Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL — the question is where that lifecycle management lives.\n\nThe bounded outcomes are:\n\n- **Outcome A — operator-based self-provisioning:** PostgreSQL lifecycle management lives in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer for Gen AI Studio, delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), following the model-registry-operator pattern. Viable only if RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for the target release.\n- **Outcome B — BFF-managed provisioning:** the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret directly when a playground with RAG is configured, modeled on how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure.\n- **Outcome C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL:** customers manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details. No provisioning code is written; this is the fallback of last resort because it degrades the out-of-the-box experience.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the go/no-go gate criteria: RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) delivery timeline assessed against the target release dates, and the sprint-planning checkpoint at which the decision must be taken.\n- Assess the operator-based option: confirm the RHOAI-controlled operator layer will be available and can carry Gen AI Studio-specific PostgreSQL lifecycle management (the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot).\n- Assess the BFF-managed option's lifecycle feasibility: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so these need explicit design answers or the option is judged unworkable.\n- Confirm per-namespace scoping as the isolation model (Auto Rag is decoupled from this strategy; per-instance scoping was only motivated by Auto Rag).\n- Confirm the portability requirement that carries into whichever implementation is selected: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the consumer must switch to the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n- Produce a decision record naming exactly one outcome, with rationale and the fallback chain.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The go/no-go gate criteria are documented (including the RHAISTRAT-7164 timeline assessment against target release dates) and the decision is recorded with rationale.\n- The BFF-managed option's lifecycle risks (cleanup on playground deletion, pod restart handling, credential rotation) are each assessed with a documented mitigation or an explicit judgment that the option is unworkable.\n- The decision names exactly one of the three outcomes and identifies the fallback chain (operator-based → BFF-managed → bring-your-own PostgreSQL).\n- The decision confirms per-namespace scoping and the portability requirement for the selected approach.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning):** this investigation determines *how* the out-of-the-box instance is provisioned; its outcome selects the provisioning implementation.\n- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping):** confirms per-namespace isolation as the selected scoping model.\n- **SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps):** the outcome choice is what protects (outcome C would sacrifice) the no-manual-setup experience.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72874,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beca486d0051d478","agent_id":"a62e85b504ccecffb","session_id":"95b0e417-f5cf-489c-8110-90a1ec4486c8","uuid":"fea9b059-8548-467d-bcf3-9e9a81b42fd4","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:41:29.798Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
