{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-15ee37b8-6885-48d8-866e-7d8f20077653","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-944d61e83fefd14b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-g6rwamj0/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E003.md","content":"# Wire the gen-ai BFF to the default pgvector vector store for playground RAG\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic wires the gen-ai BFF (in the odh-dashboard repository) so that playgrounds with RAG capabilities use the default pgvector vector store out of the box. When the BFF creates OGXServer instances for a playground, it configures the pgvector vector_io provider and consumes the connection details of the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach — replacing the current reliance on the inline vector store provider bundled in the Llama Stack distribution. It also carries the end-to-end validation, the performance benchmark against the inline Milvus baseline, and the resource sizing for the default instance.\n\nThis epic depends on the pgvector provider configuration surface delivered by the OGX-side provider integration, and on the provisioning-approach go/no-go decision (the decision determines whether the BFF consumes an operator-created connection secret, creates the PostgreSQL resources itself, or consumes a user-provided connection). Those couplings are captured in its dependency chain and acceptance criteria.\n\n## Scope\n\n- When creating OGXServer instances for playgrounds with RAG, configure the pgvector vector_io provider (the remote pgvector provider, activated via its environment guard) so RAG capabilities work without the inline provider.\n- Consume the connection details of the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach, scoped to the playground's namespace (per-namespace isolation).\n- Keep the application storage configuration as-is (SQLite default) — only the vector_io provider configuration changes; no storage migration.\n- End-to-end validation of Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows (document upload, indexing, retrieval) against the default vector store, verifying no loss of current capability.\n- Sprint-1 benchmark: capture the inline Milvus baseline and verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude. The default instance is scoped to development experimentation — no latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99), and flat scan (no index) is acceptable for typical playground RAG document sets.\n- Resource sizing for the provisioned PostgreSQL instance: start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits and adjust based on the benchmark results.\n- User guidance that production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database (the default is a development-experimentation store).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- OGXServer instances created for RAG-enabled playgrounds have the pgvector vector_io provider configured and connect to the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach in the playground's namespace.\n- Playground RAG workflows (document upload, indexing, retrieval) function end to end with no loss of current capability compared to the inline-provider experience, verified against an instance provisioned by the selected approach.\n- The benchmark report captures the inline Milvus baseline and shows pgvector within the same order of magnitude (order-of-magnitude comparison, not a latency threshold).\n- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are documented, derived from the model-registry-operator reference and adjusted per the benchmark.\n- Per-namespace isolation is verified: playgrounds in different namespaces use isolated vector store data.\n- The pgvector provider configuration is reversible at the configuration level (the wiring can be disabled or re-pointed without code rollback), providing a rollback path as the inline-provider capability is replaced.\n- User guidance documents that production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning):** the BFF wiring is what makes the provisioned instance usable by playgrounds without user setup.\n- **AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function, no loss of current capability):** the end-to-end validation and benchmark are bundled here because they exercise the same BFF/provider integration surface this epic builds — splitting them out would force two passes over the same integration and an artificial serialization boundary.\n- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation):** verified end to end in this epic's validation.\n- **AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments):** the benchmark and the documented resource sizing establish the overhead profile of the default instance.\n- **SC-1 (no functional regression when inline providers are removed) / SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps):** covered by the end-to-end, no-manual-setup validation above.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":74629,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beca486d0051d478","agent_id":"a62e85b504ccecffb","session_id":"95b0e417-f5cf-489c-8110-90a1ec4486c8","uuid":"869640b1-d8d0-459f-acc5-07ca13830aaa","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:41:55.574Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
