{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-44d68c17-1756-4be7-a281-9ca30a56b76e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-803dc4c564cf7061","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-g6rwamj0/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the gen-ai BFF\n\n## Description\n\nBranch B of the provisioning decision: if the go/no-go selects BFF-managed provisioning, this epic has the gen-ai BFF (in the odh-dashboard repository) provision the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, modeled on how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. When a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret — giving Gen AI Studio a default vector store out of the box without waiting for the RHOAI-controlled operator layer.\n\nBecause the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, pod restart handling, credential rotation) requires explicit design attention, and the implementation must be designed for portability to the platform-provided shared database planned for RHOAI 3.6.\n\n## Scope\n\n- When a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret; if one already exists, it is reused.\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector out of the box — no custom image build and no build-pipeline onboarding is required.\n- Design and implement lifecycle management: cleanup when the playground is deleted, handling of PostgreSQL pod restarts, and a documented approach to credential rotation — acknowledging the BFF has no reconciliation loop.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (planned RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n- Scope instances per namespace for multi-tenant isolation; set resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's reference sizing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Provisioning is idempotent: configuring a playground with RAG results in exactly one running pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance in the namespace, and an existing instance is reused rather than duplicated.\n- Lifecycle behaviors are validated: resources are cleaned up on playground deletion, the instance recovers from PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation is implemented or documented with its limits.\n- Portability is documented: consuming a shared platform connection string requires no rewrite of the RAG workflow path.\n- Per-namespace isolation is verified: playgrounds in different namespaces use isolated instances and vector store data.\n- The instance profile is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments, with resource requests/limits derived from the model-registry-operator reference.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning):** this branch delivers the out-of-the-box provisioning when BFF-managed provisioning is selected.\n- **AC-4 (separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance):** the PostgreSQL workload runs as its own deployment created independently of the OGX instance.\n- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation):** instances are scoped per namespace.\n- **AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments):** reference-based resource sizing.\n- **SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps):** BFF provisioning removes all user-side setup.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":76574,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"cha