{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82e6cc8b6e3ff0a0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n3\ttitle: Wire the gen-ai BFF to the default pgvector vector store for playground RAG\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the bring-your-own PostgreSQL outcome is selected (neither\n16\t    operator-based nor BFF-managed provisioning), this epic is rewritten to consume\n17\t    a user-provided PostgreSQL connection from the OGXServer CR spec instead of a\n18\t    platform-provisioned instance.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Wire the gen-ai BFF to the default pgvector vector store for playground RAG\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tThis epic wires the gen-ai BFF (in the odh-dashboard repository) so that playgrounds with RAG capabilities use the default pgvector vector store out of the box. When the BFF creates OGXServer instances for a playground, it configures the pgvector vector_io provider and consumes the connection details of the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach — replacing the current reliance on the inline vector store provider bundled in the Llama Stack distribution. It also carries the end-to-end validation, the performance benchmark against the inline Milvus baseline, and the resource sizing for the default instance.\n41\t\n42\tThis epic depends on the pgvector provider configuration surface delivered by the OGX-side provider integration, and on the provisioning-approach go/no-go decision (the decision determines whether the BFF consumes an operator-created connection secret, creates the PostgreSQL resources itself, or consumes a user-provided connection). Those couplings are captured in its dependency chain and acceptance criteria.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- When creating OGXServer instances for playgrounds with RAG, configure the pgvector vector_io provider (the remote pgvector provider, activated via its environment guard) so RAG capabilities work without the inline provider.\n47\t- Consume the connection details of the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach, scoped to the playground's namespace (per-namespace isolation).\n48\t- Keep the application storage configuration as-is (SQLite default) — only the vector_io provider configuration changes; no storage migration.\n49\t- End-to-end validation of Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows (document upload, indexing, retrieval) against the default vector store, verifying no loss of current capability.\n50\t- Sprint-1 benchmark: capture the inline Milvus baseline and verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude. The default instance is scoped to development experimentation — no latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99), and flat scan (no index) is acceptable for typical playground RAG document sets.\n51\t- Resource sizing for the provisioned PostgreSQL instance: start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits and adjust based on the benchmark results.\n52\t- User guidance that production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database (the default is a development-experimentation store).\n53\t\n54\t## Acceptance Criteria\n55\t\n56\t- OGXServer instances created for RAG-enabled playgrounds have the pgvector vector_io provider configured and connect to the PostgreSQL instance provisioned by the selected provisioning approach in the playground's namespace.\n57\t- Playground RAG workflows (document upload, indexing, retrieval) function end to end with no loss of current capability compared to the inline-provider experience, verified against an instance provisioned by the selected approach.\n58\t- The benchmark report captures the inline Milvus baseline and shows pgvector within the same order of magnitude (order-of-magnitude comparison, not a latency threshold).\n59\t- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are documented, derived from the model-registry-operator reference and adjusted per the benchmark.\n60\t- Per-namespace isolation is verified: playgrounds in different namespaces use isolated vector store data.\n61\t- The pgvector provider configuration is reversible at the configuration level (the wiring can be disabled or re-pointed without code rollback), providing a rollback path as the inline-provider capability is replaced.\n62\t- User guidance documents that production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability\n65\t\n66\t- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning):** the BFF wiring is what makes the provisioned instance usable by playgrounds without user setup.\n67\t- **AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function, no loss of current capability):** the end-to-end validation and benchmark are bundled here because they exercise the same BFF/provider integration surface this epic builds — splitting them out would force two passes over the same integration and an artificial serialization boundary.\n68\t- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation):** verified end to end in this epic's validation.\n69\t- **AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments):** the benchmark and the documented resource sizing establish the overhead profile of the default instance.\n70\t- **SC-1 (no functional regression when inline providers are removed) / SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps):** covered by the end-to-end, no-manual-setup validation above.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-be1bb409803129b1","agent_id":"a56f480245a5b00a0","session_id":"95b0e417-f5cf-489c-8110-90a1ec4486c8","uuid":"38603593-a04e-4569-b9ce-ff09f7007dd9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:44:03.197Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-82e6cc8b6e3ff0a0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
