{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a774a0bbce12eb9c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'external_dependency is scored inconsistently across sibling epics\n10\t    for the same condition: BRANCH-B-E004 scores +1 (no upstream/vendor dependency)\n11\t    while E002, E003, and BRANCH-A-E004 score 0 with rationales that also state no\n12\t    upstream/vendor dependency exists. Per the rubric (+1 = none, -1 = upstream/vendor\n13\t    needed), E002''s 0 contradicts its own rationale. Correction: re-score external_dependency\n14\t    to +1 on epics whose rationale states no external dependency (or record a uniform\n15\t    justification for 0, e.g. the internal RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery dependency, on\n16\t    all of them).'\n17\terror: null\n18\t---\n19\t## Review Summary\n20\t\n21\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n22\tRecommendation: accept\n23\t\n24\tContract validator: OK (exit 0 — body sections, canonical component names, docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all pass). Triage: null (full decomposition); epic_count=5 with conditional branches is the expected shape. The decomposition correctly treats the Staff Engineer Input as authoritative over the AI-generated strategy body, documents every override in a traceability table, and models the RHAISTRAT-7164 timing risk as a go/no-go Investigation with conditional branches rather than a blocking edge across all work — the structurally correct shape for this strategy.\n25\t\n26\t## Criterion Details\n27\t\n28\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n29\tHLR set = the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (AC-1..AC-6) plus Success Criteria (SC-1..SC-3); the strategy's own HLR table is in the unavailable attachment, and this substitution is recorded as a Health Warning. AC-1, AC-2, AC-4, AC-5, AC-6, SC-1, SC-2, SC-3 all map to at least one epic, with the branch epics correctly listed as alternatives (\"exactly one is instantiated\"). AC-3 (Auto Rag) is descoped with explicit justification — the Staff Engineer Input directly instructs removing Auto Rag from acceptance criteria (it moves to RHAISTRAT-7824 / RHOAI 3.6), so this is an authoritative override, not a coverage gap; it is flagged both in the matrix and in Health Warnings. All epics are P0 matching the Blocker strategy priority — no priority inheritance errors. The AC-2 bundling (E2E validation + benchmark folded into E003) is priority-neutral (all P0) and is recorded with inline rationale in the matrix row, satisfying the coupling-rationale requirement. Traceability matrix present and complete.\n30\t\n31\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n32\tFrontmatter/DAG cross-check: every diagram edge has a matching `dependencies` entry and vice versa (E003 deps [E001, E002]; both branch epics deps [E001]); arrows run dependency → dependent per convention. No cycles. No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic — E003's coupling to the selected provisioning implementation is correctly expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate (\"verified against an instance provisioned by the selected approach\"), matching the branch rule. Both branch files set `branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`. The E001 → E003 edge is a true gate (gated_by set, rewrite-on-failure impact populated). The E002 → E003 edge is justified by Rule 13: E002's deliverable explicitly includes the documented provider configuration surface the BFF builds against. The absent E002 → branch-epic edges are correct — the provisioned PostgreSQL is consumed as a connection/secret reference (configuration reference), not built against. No konflux-onboarding or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not applicable. Critical path length 2 is appropriate for the post-descoping strategy size (staff input eliminated the image build and Konflux onboarding work).\n33\t\n34\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n35\tComponent/team tuples are clean: OGX Core owns E002 (the only OGX-side work); Gen AI Studio owns E001, E003, and both branch epics. Different tuples produce separate epics. The two branch epics are conditional alternatives (exactly one instantiated), not scope duplication. Resource sizing appears in both E003 and the branch epics, but complementarily (branch sets initial sizing from the model-registry-operator reference; E003 adjusts per benchmark) and this split is documented in the AC-6 matrix row — not independent construction of the same deliverable. E003 bundles BFF wiring + E2E validation + benchmark with the coupling rationale recorded in the matrix row; these exercise the same integration surface, so the bundle is the compliant shape. No epic exceeds the ~2-week guideline — E002 is explicitly scoped small per the staff input that the provider integration is largely reusable. The single user-guidance AC in E003 (production-scale users should use an external vector database) is a one-line deliverable directed by the Staff Engineer Input, not a content-authoring work stream — acceptable.\n36\t\n37\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n38\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome determines which branch epic exists and how E003 consumes the connection, i.e., it changes downstream structure. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Gating metadata is fully consistent: every epic listing E001 in `dependencies` (E003, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004) sets `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, every `gated_by` target is in that epic's direct `dependencies` list, and every `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and a substantive `fallback_approach` (E003: rewrite to bring-your-own; branch A: remove with fallback chain; branch B: rewrite to bring-your-own). E002 correctly has no gate — the investigation does not change what it builds.\n39\t\n40\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n41\tSignal sets are type-correct (E001 uses `investigation_signals`; all Implementation epics use `ai_signals`), rationale files exist for all five epics, and E001's five values match the investigation rubric precisely (notably `source_accessibility: 0` — the RHAISTRAT-7164 timing answer does not yet exist in any readable artifact — and `human_judgment_required: -2` — the gating question is a sprint-planning decision). `architecture_claims: 1` rationales cite specific `.context/architecture-context` files (ogx-distribution.md, odh-dashboard.md, model-registry-operator.md), passing the calibration check. One calibration inconsistency: `external_dependency` is scored 0 on E002/E003/BRANCH-A-E004 with rationales stating no upstream/vendor dependency exists, while BRANCH-B-E004 scores the identical condition +1. Per the rubric (+1 = none, -1 = upstream/vendor needed), E002's 0 — backed by a rationale flatly stating none is identified — contradicts its own rationale; E003/BRANCH-A's 0 is defensible via the internal RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery dependency but should be justified uniformly. The error is in the conservative direction and does not change any epic's implementability tier.\n42\t\n43\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n44\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E003 (the inline-provider replacement) carries an explicit reversibility/rollback AC (\"reversible at the configuration level... without code rollback\"); no konflux or docs-authoring epics exist, so those mandates are inapplicable. No externally-controlled precondition ACs — conditions are on what each epic builds or verifies (idempotent provisioning, isolation verified, benchmark report captured, decision record naming exactly one outcome). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; cross-epic coupling is expressed via full epic IDs in frontmatter and via acceptance-criterion gates.\n45\t\n46\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n47\tAll