{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfda13dc92cd124a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 1\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation decides how the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio playground RAG is provisioned in RHOAI 3.5, so that a default vector store is available out-of-the-box when inline providers are removed from the Llama Stack distribution. It defines the go/no-go gate criteria (as part of sprint planning), assesses the feasibility of each candidate approach, and produces a decision record that determines which provisioning implementation proceeds. A platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC will not be available until RHOAI 3.6, so for 3.5 Gen AI Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL — the question is where that lifecycle management lives.\n30\t\n31\tThe bounded outcomes are:\n32\t\n33\t- **Outcome A — operator-based self-provisioning:** PostgreSQL lifecycle management lives in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer for Gen AI Studio, delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), following the model-registry-operator pattern. Viable only if RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for the target release.\n34\t- **Outcome B — BFF-managed provisioning:** the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret directly when a playground with RAG is configured, modeled on how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure.\n35\t- **Outcome C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL:** customers manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details. No provisioning code is written; this is the fallback of last resort because it degrades the out-of-the-box experience.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\t- Define the go/no-go gate criteria: RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) delivery timeline assessed against the target release dates, and the sprint-planning checkpoint at which the decision must be taken.\n40\t- Assess the operator-based option: confirm the RHOAI-controlled operator layer will be available and can carry Gen AI Studio-specific PostgreSQL lifecycle management (the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot).\n41\t- Assess the BFF-managed option's lifecycle feasibility: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so these need explicit design answers or the option is judged unworkable.\n42\t- Confirm per-namespace scoping as the isolation model (Auto Rag is decoupled from this strategy; per-instance scoping was only motivated by Auto Rag).\n43\t- Confirm the portability requirement that carries into whichever implementation is selected: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the consumer must switch to the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n44\t- Produce a decision record naming exactly one outcome, with rationale and the fallback chain.\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- The go/no-go gate criteria are documented (including the RHAISTRAT-7164 timeline assessment against target release dates) and the decision is recorded with rationale.\n49\t- The BFF-managed option's lifecycle risks (cleanup on playground deletion, pod restart handling, credential rotation) are each assessed with a documented mitigation or an explicit judgment that the option is unworkable.\n50\t- The decision names exactly one of the three outcomes and identifies the fallback chain (operator-based → BFF-managed → bring-your-own PostgreSQL).\n51\t- The decision confirms per-namespace scoping and the portability requirement for the selected approach.\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability\n54\t\n55\t- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning):** this investigation determines *how* the out-of-the-box instance is provisioned; its outcome selects the provisioning implementation.\n56\t- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping):** confirms per-namespace isolation as the selected scoping model.\n57\t- **SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps):** the outcome choice is what protects (outcome C would sacrifice) the no-manual-setup experience.\n58\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fab0a6e3589f71b","agent_id":"aa3d9f1d9fb68bac1","session_id":"95b0e417-f5cf-489c-8110-90a1ec4486c8","uuid":"c9ceceba-7662-4aae-947e-27db8d1d870a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:50:23.119Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfda13dc92cd124a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
