{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a708b02aaf4f9c4b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the gen-ai BFF\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: 'If BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable (cleanup\n16\t    on playground deletion, pod restarts, credential rotation), fall back to requiring\n17\t    customers to bring their own PostgreSQL: users manually configure the OGXServer\n18\t    CR spec with their PostgreSQL connection details.'\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the gen-ai BFF\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tBranch B of the provisioning decision: if the go/no-go selects BFF-managed provisioning, this epic has the gen-ai BFF (in the odh-dashboard repository) provision the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, modeled on how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. When a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret — giving Gen AI Studio a default vector store out of the box without waiting for the RHOAI-controlled operator layer.\n40\t\n41\tBecause the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, pod restart handling, credential rotation) requires explicit design attention, and the implementation must be designed for portability to the platform-provided shared database planned for RHOAI 3.6.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- When a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret; if one already exists, it is reused.\n46\t- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector out of the box — no custom image build and no build-pipeline onboarding is required.\n47\t- Design and implement lifecycle management: cleanup when the playground is deleted, handling of PostgreSQL pod restarts, and a documented approach to credential rotation — acknowledging the BFF has no reconciliation loop.\n48\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (planned RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n49\t- Scope instances per namespace for multi-tenant isolation; set resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's reference sizing.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- Provisioning is idempotent: configuring a playground with RAG results in exactly one running pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance in the namespace, and an existing instance is reused rather than duplicated.\n54\t- Lifecycle behaviors are validated: resources are cleaned up on playground deletion, the instance recovers from PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation is implemented or documented with its limits.\n55\t- Portability is documented: consuming a shared platform connection string requires no rewrite of the RAG workflow path.\n56\t- Per-namespace isolation is verified: playgrounds in different namespaces use isolated instances and vector store data.\n57\t- The instance profile is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments, with resource requests/limits derived from the model-registry-operator reference.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning):** this branch delivers the out-of-the-box provisioning when BFF-managed provisioning is selected.\n62\t- **AC-4 (separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance):** the PostgreSQL workload runs as its own deployment created independently of the OGX instance.\n63\t- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation):** instances are scoped per namespace.\n64\t- **AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments):** reference-based resource sizing.\n65\t- **SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps):** BFF provisioning removes all user-side setup.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fab0a6e3589f71b","agent_id":"aa3d9f1d9fb68bac1","session_id":"95b0e417-f5cf-489c-8110-90a1ec4486c8","uuid":"6e937ec4-1809-4902-9f20-009e9421f999","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:50:27.815Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a708b02aaf4f9c4b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
